     ccoonnvv converts _n_u_m_b_e_r and prints it as a hexadecimal,
     decimal, octal, and binary value, followed by its low-order
     byte as a quoted character, all on one line.  If no _n_u_m_b_e_r
     is given on the command line, ccoonnvv reads lines from the
     standard input and converts each nonblank line the same way,
     until end of file.

     _n_u_m_b_e_r may be written with an optional leading - for a nega-
     tive value, followed by one of the prefixes below:

          00xx or 00XX    a hexadecimal value
          #           also introduces a hexadecimal value
          00           (no following x or X) introduces an octal value
          $           introduces a binary value

     A prefix consisting of a quote character followed by one
     other character gives the ASCII code of that other character
     as the value.

     With none of the above prefixes, the number is read as
     decimal.  Embedded blanks and tabs within the digits are ig-
     nored; hexadecimal digits may be given in either case.

     In the character field of the output, ccoonnvv prints the
     value's low-order byte in quotes.  If the byte's high bit is
     set, ccoonnvv prints a leading ~ and clears the bit before prin-
     ting the character.  If the resulting code is a control
     character or DEL, ccoonnvv prints a leading ^ and the correspon-
     ding visible letter (DEL is shown as ^?).

